<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">
  <!--
    The search engine's SAFETY suite: what the output layer does to the disk, to memory and to a
    hard kill. It is deliberately a separate project from tests\SeedLab.Search.Tests, which answers
    "is the answer right". That suite's later sections do build sessions and run the preflight, and
    one runs a scan with no sink. Its section 14 (2026-09-24) is the one exception: to prove where a
    funnel's stage 2 checkpoints, it writes a bounded results file and resumes one stage-2 checkpoint
    from disk, and it runs vseed itself. None of its checks hard-kills a run or exercises the bounds,
    rotation, reduction and ceilings below, so it would still pass with nearly every fix in this suite
    reverted. (This comment used to say "every one of its 70 checks constructs SeedEvaluator,
    CompiledQuery, ScanPlan or Presets directly"; it has grown past 300 since, and the gap it names is
    still open.)
    That gap is what decisions item 9 calls a tripwire that is not tested, and this project is it.

    It is a console program with subcommands rather than a single run, because two of the things it
    proves can only be proved by killing a process:

      proof bounded    the keep setting is a real cap: the file holds exactly N records and they are exactly
                       the top N an unbounded run writes, seed for seed
      proof memory     a whole-space plan (the whole int32 range) is enumerated lazily and the block queue is bounded
      proof run        one leg of a killable run (used by killtest.ps1, not usually run by hand)
      proof rotate     1 GB-style segments, gzip, manifest, and reduce-then-delete; add the csv switch to
                       check that every segment carries its own header
      proof refuse     every refusal: non-mergeable reductions, a rotated JSON array, resuming a
                       rotated run, an all-must-have bounded query, and the 100 %-match warning
      proof policy     the per-goal grid policy over every shipped preset
      proof region     the measured cost of a region restriction at several radii
      proof estimate   the live projection against what the run really did
      proof screen     screen-then-verify returns the same seeds AND the same records as a pure
                       12 m run - the check that makes the whole mechanism trustworthy
      proof blocks     a completed run's results file is the same bytes at every block size (jsonl,
                       json, csv; keep N and keep all), and a run resumed on another thread count at
                       the automatic size keeps its checkpoint's size and finishes with the
                       uninterrupted run's bytes - what makes a per-run block size safe

      killtest.ps1     hard-kills a run at three points and resumes it, then compares SHA-256 with
                       an uninterrupted run. Run it twice: bare, and with the KeepAll switch; add
                       AutoBlock and ResumeThreads to resume at the automatic size on another thread count.

    Exit code is 0 when the subcommand's own checks pass.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <AssemblyName>proof</AssemblyName>
    <RootNamespace>Proof</RootNamespace>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>disable</ImplicitUsings>
    <InvariantGlobalization>true</InvariantGlobalization>
    <ServerGarbageCollection>true</ServerGarbageCollection>
    <ConcurrentGarbageCollection>false</ConcurrentGarbageCollection>
  </PropertyGroup>
  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\src\SeedLab.Search\SeedLab.Search.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\src\SeedLab.Seeds\SeedLab.Seeds.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>
</Project>
